<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cicero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">106–<a href="/years/43-bce">43 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Marcus Tullius Cicero</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cicero (Marcus Tullius Cicero) was a Roman statesman, lawyer, orator and philosopher, the greatest master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> prose. As consul in 63 BCE he suppressed the conspiracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catiline">Catiline</a>, and he later opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>. His speeches, letters and philosophical works, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tusculan-disputations">Tusculan Disputations</a>, are a principal source for the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>; he was killed on Antony's orders in 43 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cicero.jpg" alt="Cicero" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Cicero_(1st-cent._BC)_-_Palazzo_Nuovo_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
